**Root cause & fix:**

Since this project uses **Vue 3**, the Proxy-based reactivity system automatically tracks direct property assignments on reactive objects (`this.obj[key] = value`) — no `$set` workaround is needed. All `this.$set(...)` calls have been replaced with plain direct assignments (e.g. `this.branchesByProject[id] = branches`), which Vue 3 handles correctly out of the box.

## Analysis

The root cause is a **reactivity timing issue** in `ChangesPanel.vue`. When the component first loads with cached data, `selectedProjectId` is set at the end of `loadAllProjects()`, but by that point `cachedRepoChanges` is computed from `changesByProject` which was populated using **direct property assignment** on a nested object — Vue's reactivity system doesn't always detect this on first render.

The flow:
1. `loadAllProjects()` runs
2. `this.changesByProject[key] = changes` — Vue may not detect this deep mutation
3. `this.selectedProjectId = first ? this.projId(first) : null` — triggers computed `cachedRepoChanges`
4. But `cachedRepoChanges` returns `null` because Vue didn't track the `changesByProject[key]` assignment reactively

When user **changes project**, `selectedProjectId` watcher fires → `loadChangesForProject()` runs → again assigns `this.changesByProject[key] = changes`, but now Vue is already tracking the key so it works.

## Fix

Use `$set`-style replacement (reassign the whole object) to ensure reactivity when populating `changesByProject`:

